<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">&amp;c.</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">etc.</reg></choice> <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">also</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">Also</reg></choice> held a <lb/><ref pm:link-pointer-type="topic" target="x4071">conference</ref> in the <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Evening</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">evening.</reg></choice> <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Br <lb/>Jese</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">Brothers
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per497" n="Gause, Jesse">Jesse
Gause</ref></reg></choice> and <persName>Mogan</persName> and
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per81" n="William E. McLellin"><choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">William
<lb/>M<hi rend="superscript">c</hi>lelen</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice> <supplied resp="WordConversion">William E.
McLellin</supplied></ref> <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">was</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">were</reg></choice> excommunicated <lb/>from the
church<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded"> &amp;c—</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">, etc.</reg></choice><note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e1276" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">“Jese” is probably
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per497" n="Jesse Gause">Jesse
Gause</ref>,
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref>’s counselor in the church presidency, who “denied
the faith” sometime earlier in <date when-iso="1832">1832</date>.<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e1287" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X3205" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Minute Book 2, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="X07868286-8268-42D4-BE22-10A20BCD08F9">30
Apr. 1832</ref></abbr></ref>; see also
<ref target="X9916" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Quinn, “<ref pm:link-pointer-type="web" target="http://byustudies.byu.edu/content/jesse-gause-joseph-smiths-little-known-counselor">Jesse
Gause</ref>,” 492</abbr></ref>.</note> JS became increasingly frustrated with
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per81" n="William E. McLellin">McLellin</ref> throughout <date when-iso="1832">1832</date>. After McLellin failed to carry out a mission in
the eastern states with JS’s brother
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per65" n="Samuel Harrison Smith">Samuel</ref>, a <date when-iso="1832-01">January
1832</date> revelation rebuked him for the “murmurings of his heart” and
instructed him to serve a mission in the southern states,<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e1316" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X5388" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x1471">Revelation, 25 Jan.
1832</ref>, in Doctrine and Covenants 87:2, 1835 ed.
[D&amp;C 75:6–8]</ref>.</note> which he abandoned as well.<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e1319" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X0503" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x123">JS, Greenville, IN, to
Emma Smith, Kirtland, OH, 6 June 1832</ref>, Manuscripts about Mormons
at Chicago History Museum, Research Center, Chicago Historical
Society</abbr></ref>.</note> Soon thereafter, JS rebuked the
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc140" n="Missouri United States">Missouri</ref> Latter-day Saints for accepting
McLellin into their “fellowship &amp; communion.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e1332" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X7151" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x125">JS, Hyrum [Hiram, OH], to
William W. Phelps, Zion [Independence], MO, 31 July 1832</ref>, JS Collection,
CHL</abbr></ref>.</note></note>
