                <reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Bretheren</reg>
                <reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">brethren</reg>
              </choice> in <ref pm:link-pointer-type="topic" target="x4116">Zion</ref>
              <lb/>for Christ<choice>
                <reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded"> Sake</reg>
                <reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">’s sake.</reg>
              </choice> Amen<reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">.</reg></p> 
<ab xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4858" rend="noindent" type="journal-entry-divider"><date when-iso="1834-01-11">11 January 1834</date> • Saturday</ab>
<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4860" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref> apparently penned the above
<date when-iso="1834-01-16">16 January</date> entry during his trip away from
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc13" n="Kirtland Geauga Ohio United States">Kirtland</ref>. On JS’s return,
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per47" n="Oliver Cowdery">Oliver
Cowdery</ref> apparently used notes taken at <date when-iso="1834-01-11">the
previous Saturday</date>’s<note type="errata" resp="th" pm:link-target-type="text" xml:id="X642E37DE-8B72-4C88-91CE-0EC5C46AA350">Changed “Sunday’s” to
“Saturday’s”.<note type="comment-external" corresp="X642E37DE-8B72-4C88-91CE-0EC5C46AA350"/></note> prayer meeting to
compose this entry.</note><p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4877" rend="indent"><handShift resp="seniels" scribe="per47"/><note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4879" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote-scribe"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Smith, Joseph, Jr.">JS</ref>
                handwriting ends; <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per47" n="Oliver Cowdery">Oliver Cowdery</ref> begins.</note><choice>
                <reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded"><date when-iso="1834-01-11">January 11,
                    1834</date>. <lb/></reg>
                <reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/>
              </choice>This evening <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">Joseph Smith Jr.</ref>, <lb/><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per63" n="Frederick G. Williams">Frederick G. Williams</ref>, <lb/><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per222" n="Newel K. Whitney">Newel K.
                Whitney</ref>, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per556" n="John Johnson">John <lb/>Johnson</ref>, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per47" n="Oliver Cowdery">Oliver Cowdery</ref>, <lb/>and <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per79" n="Orson Hyde">Orson Hyde</ref> united <lb/>in prayer<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4916" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref>, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per63" n="Frederick G. Williams">Williams</ref>, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per222" n="Newel K. Whitney">Whitney</ref>, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per556" n="John Johnson">Johnson</ref>, and <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per75" n="Sidney Rigdon">Sidney Rigdon</ref>
                (not present on this occasion) constituted the <ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc13" n="Kirtland Geauga Ohio United States">Kirtland</ref> branch of the
                United Firm. <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per47" n="Oliver Cowdery">Cowdery</ref>, a member of the <ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc140" n="Missouri United States">Missouri</ref> branch of the firm, was appointed four
                months earlier to represent the other members of that branch.<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4942" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X8611" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Backman, <title>Heavens
                        Resound,</title> 71</abbr></ref>; <ref target="X3989" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Minute Book 1,
                        <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="X5F3C0551-6821-461B-A6AC-B243178931D5">11 Sept.
                      1833</ref></abbr></ref>.</note>
                <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per79" n="Orson Hyde">Hyde</ref>,
                appointed a clerk to the church presidency in <date when-iso="1833-06">June
                  1833</date>, had recently returned from Missouri with the report of the brewing
                violence there.<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4967" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X3989" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Minute Book 1,
                        <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="X6B33D0FB-D4D4-4580-A1B1-AC5429389872">6
                        June
                      1833</ref></abbr></ref>;
                  see also JS, Journal, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="JSPPJ1_d1e3678">25
                    Nov. 1833</ref>.</note> The economic interests of the firm were reflected in
                their prayer concerning church debts, the printing operation, and the well-being of
                the Missouri Latter-day Saints. </note> and asked the <lb/>Lord to grant the
              fol<lb/>lowing petition: <pb xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4988" type="present" n="43" facs="6662_MS155_1_01_JSJ-1_043.jpg"/></p> 
<p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e4990" rend="indent">Firstly, <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">That</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">that</reg></choice> the Lord would <lb/>grant that our lives might
<lb/>be precious in his sight, that <lb/>he would watch over our per<lb/>sons
and give his angels cha<lb/>rge concerning us and our <lb/>families<reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">,</reg> that no evil nor un<lb/>seen hand might
be permitted <lb/>to harm us.</p> <p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5010" rend="indent">Secondly, <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">That</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">that</reg></choice> the Lord would <lb/>also hold the lives of all
<lb/>the <ref pm:link-pointer-type="topic" target="x4112">United Firm</ref>,
and not <lb/>suffer that any of them shall <lb/>be taken.</p> 
<p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5025" rend="indent">Thirdly, <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">That</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">that</reg></choice> the Lord would <lb/>grant that our brother
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">Jo<lb/>seph</ref> might prevail over
<pb xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5034" type="present" n="44" facs="6662_MS155_1_01_JSJ-1_044.jpg"/> his enemy, even
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="x4278" n="Hurlbut, Doctor Philastus"><choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Docter P. </reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice><supplied resp="WordConversion">Doctor
Philastus</supplied> Hurl<lb/>but</ref>,<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5044" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="x4278" n="Hurlbut, Doctor Philastus">Doctor Philastus Hurlbut</ref>, a former
Latter-day Saint, had worked vigorously to discredit
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref>
and the church. During <date from-iso="1833-06" to-iso="1833-09">summer
1833</date>, JS noted that the church was “suffering great persicution on
account of” Hurlbut, who was “lieing in a wonderful manner and the peopl are
running after him and giveing him mony to brake down mormanism which much
endangers our lives.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5054" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X7151" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x179">JS, Kirtland, OH, to
William W. Phelps et al., Independence, MO, 18 Aug. 1833</ref>, JS Collection,
CHL</abbr></ref>.</note> In <date from-iso="1833-06" to-iso="1833-12">summer
and autumn 1833</date>, Hurlbut traveled in
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="x5092" n="Ohio, United States">Ohio</ref>, <placeName>Massachusetts</placeName>,
<placeName>Pennsylvania</placeName>, and
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc96" n="New York United States">New
York</ref> to collect statements against JS by his former neighbors and to
build a case that the Book of Mormon had been copied from a work of fiction
written by <persName>Solomon Spalding</persName>. On his return in
<date from-iso="1833-12-11" to-iso="1833-12-20">mid-December</date>, Hurlbut
defamed JS in lectures and stirred up further persecution.<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5085" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X0578" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Winchester, <title>Origin of the Spaulding
Story,</title> 9–11</abbr></ref>.</note> Antagonism against the church in Ohio
grew to such proportions that Mormon
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per212" n="Heber C. Kimball">Heber
C. Kimball</ref> reported, “Our enemies were raging and threatening destruction
upon us, and we had to guard night after night, and for weeks were not
permitted to take off our clothes, and were obliged to lay with our fire locks
in our arms.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5101" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X0562" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Kimball, “History,” 11</abbr></ref>; see
also <ref target="X2105" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Grua, “<ref pm:link-pointer-type="web" target="http://byustudies.byu.edu/content/joseph-smith-and-1834-d-p-hurlbut-case">Joseph Smith and
the 1834 D. P. Hurlbut Case</ref>,” 35–38</abbr></ref>.</note></note> who has
threatened his <lb/>life, whom
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr."><choice><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Expanded">brother</reg><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Clear">Brother</reg></choice> Joseph</ref> <lb/>has
<add resp="WordConversion">caused to be</add> taken with a precept;<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5128" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">A
“command in writing by a Justice of Peace, or other Officer, for bringing a
person or records before him.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5130" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X5230" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>“Precept,” in
<title>Law-Dictionary,</title> 5:271</abbr></ref>.</note>
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="x4278" n="Hurlbut, Doctor Philastus">Hurlbut</ref> threatened
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref>’s life. JS’s cousin
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per52" n="George A. Smith">George A.
Smith</ref> later recalled that “in delivering lectures he [Hurlbut] had said
he would wash his hands in Joseph Smith’s blood.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5151" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X6800" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>George A. Smith, in <title>Journal of
Discourses</title>, 15 Nov. 1864, 11:8</abbr></ref>.</note>
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc13" n="Kirtland Geauga Ohio United States">Kirtland</ref> justice of the peace
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="x4811" n="Dowen, John C.">John
Dowen</ref> later claimed that when Hurlbut said he would “kill” JS, “he meant
he would kill Mormonism.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5170" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X7921" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>John C. Dowen, Statement, 2 Jan. 1885, 3,
Manuscripts about Mormons at Chicago History Museum, Research Center, Chicago
Historical Society</abbr></ref>.</note> On <date when-iso="1833-12-21">21
December 1833</date>, JS filed a complaint with the Kirtland justice of the
peace, whose decision in a preliminary hearing stated that JS “had reason to
fear that Doctor P. Hurlbut would Beat wound or kill him.”<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5182" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X4062" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Geauga Co., OH, Court of Common Pleas,
Court Records, 1807–1904, vol. P, p. 432, 31 Mar. 1834, microfilm 20,278, U.S.
and Canada Record Collection, FHL</abbr></ref>.</note> The justice of the peace
then issued a warrant for Hurlbut’s arrest.<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5191" type="toe-note" resp="s" pm:link-target-type="text"><ref target="X7921" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Dowen, Statement, 2 Jan. 1885,
3</abbr></ref>.</note> This legal action was intended to impede Hurlbut from
carrying out the threat. Preliminary evaluation of JS’s complaint against
Hurlbut began two days later, on <date when-iso="1834-01-13">13 January</date>.
<!--On the
                                outcome, see 27n78 and 38n127 herein. --><!--On the outcome, see JS, Journal, 28 Jan. 1834, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="JSPPJ1_d1e5716" xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:pm="https://xmlref.com/local/pm" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xhtml="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:lds="https://ldsc.xmlref.com/local/ldschurch">footnote</ref> and 7–9 Apr. 1834, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="JSPPJ1_d1e8273">footnote</ref>.--></note>
<lb/>that the Lord would fill <lb/>the heart of the <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Court</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">court</reg></choice> with <lb/>a spirit to do justice<choice><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Expanded">,</reg><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Clear"/></choice> and <lb/>cause that the law of the <lb/>land may be
magnified <lb/>in bringing him to justice.</p> 
<p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5219" rend="indent">Fourthly, <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">That</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">that</reg></choice> the Lord would <lb/>provide, in the order of
his <lb/><choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Providence</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">providence</reg></choice>, the
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per222" n="Whitney, Newel Kimball">bishop</ref> of <lb/>this <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Church</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">church</reg></choice><note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5234" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per222" n="Newel K. Whitney">Newel K.
Whitney</ref>.</note> with means <lb/>sufficient to discharge <lb/>every debt
that the Firm <lb/>owes,<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5245" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">Part of the firm’s debt
stemmed from the purchase of the
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="x5000" n="“French Farm,” Kirtland, Kirtland Township, Geauga (now in Lake) County, Ohio, United States">French
farm</ref>—the property upon which the
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="x5077" n="House of the Lord, Kirtland, Kirtland Township, Geauga (now in Lake) County, Ohio, United States">House
of the Lord</ref> was being built. In the coming months, efforts were made to
retire church debts. On <date when-iso="1834-02-20">20 February 1834</date>, a
church council commissioned
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per79" n="Orson Hyde">Orson
Hyde</ref> and
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per123" n="Orson Pratt">Orson
Pratt</ref> to raise funds to retire this debt. (<ref target="X3989" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Minute Book 1, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="X5EF454CC-718C-4BAF-88B0-7DA0A0539A4B">20
Feb. 1834</ref></abbr></ref>.)</note> in due season,
that
<pb xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5271" type="present" n="45" facs="6662_MS155_1_01_JSJ-1_045.jpg"/> the <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Church</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">church</reg></choice> may not be <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">braught</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">brought</reg></choice> <lb/>into disrepute<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">,</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice> and the <choice><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Expanded">saints</reg><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Clear">Saints</reg></choice> <lb/>be afflicted by the hands of their
<lb/>enemies.</p> <p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5281" rend="indent">Fifthly,
<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">That</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">that</reg></choice> the Lord would <lb/>protect our printing press
<lb/>from the hands of evil men<choice><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Expanded">,</reg><reg resp="DarowskiJF" type="Clear"/></choice><note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5287" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">In
a letter to
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per77" n="Edward Partridge">Edward
Partridge</ref> and others,
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref>
outlined <ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per47" n="Oliver Cowdery">Oliver Cowdery</ref>’s difficulties returning from
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc35" n="New York New York County New York United States">New York City</ref> with a
press and type, “<hi rend="underscore">hauling</hi> them up in the midst of
<hi rend="underscore">mobs.</hi>” (<ref target="X9344" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x210">JS, Kirtland, OH, to
Edward Partridge et al., Clay Co., MO, 30 Mar. 1834</ref>,
in Cowdery, Letterbook, 30–36</abbr></ref>.)</note> <lb/>and give us means to
send <lb/>forth his word, even his gos<lb/>pel<reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">,</reg> that the ears of all <lb/>may hear it, and also that
<lb/>we may print his scrip<lb/>tures;<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5329" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">Publication of a
compilation of revelations, the
<ref target="X8749" pm:link-pointer-type="source">Book of Commandments</ref>,
ceased when a mob destroyed the printing office in
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc140" n="Missouri United States">Missouri</ref>. The mandate to publish the
revelations was explicit. A new compilation, the
<ref target="X9DBE" pm:link-pointer-type="source">Doctrine and Covenants</ref>,
was published in <date when-iso="1835">1835</date>. (See <ref target="d2e66961" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="X7203">Revelation, 4 Dec.
1831</ref>, in Doctrine and Covenants 89:4, 1835 ed. [D&amp;C 72:20–23]</ref>;
and <ref target="X3205" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Minute Book 2, <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="XF261CD81-E5D4-4CE7-8792-06C319FB9773">12
Nov. 1831</ref>
and <ref pm:link-pointer-type="text" target="X07868286-8268-42D4-BE22-10A20BCD08F9">30
Apr. 1832</ref></abbr></ref>.)</note> and also that he
<lb/>would give those who <lb/>were appointed to con<lb/>duct the
press<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">,</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice><note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5352" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">A “Literary Firm” had been
appointed to conduct the press. Its active members at this time were
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per46" n="Joseph Smith Jr.">JS</ref>,
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per47" n="Oliver Cowdery">Cowdery</ref>,
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per75" n="Sidney Rigdon">Sidney
Rigdon</ref>, and
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="per63" n="Frederick G. Williams">Williams</ref>. (<ref target="X5112" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><abbr>Cook, <title>Law of Consecration,</title>
43–44</abbr></ref>.)</note> wisdom <lb/>sufficient that the cause
<pb xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5380" type="present" n="46" facs="6662_MS155_1_01_JSJ-1_046.jpg"/> may not be hindered, but <lb/>that men’s
eyes may thereby <lb/>be opened to see the truth.</p> 
<p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5388" rend="indent">Sixthly, <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">That</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">that</reg></choice> the Lord would <lb/>deliver
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="topic" target="x4116">Zion</ref>, and
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="topic" target="x4084">gather</ref> in <lb/>his
scattered people<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">,</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice> <lb/>to
possess it in peace;<note xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5403" pm:link-target-type="text" type="editorial-footnote">Less than a month earlier, a revelation promised that
the
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc140" n="Missouri United States">Missouri</ref> Latter-day Saints would yet possess
their lands in
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="place" target="plc57" n="Jackson County Missouri United States">Jackson County</ref>: “Zion shall not
be moved out of her place, notwithstanding her children are scattered, they
that remain and are pure in heart shall return and come to their inheritances.”
This echoed an earlier revelation that stated, “Zion shall be redeemed,
although she is chastened for a little season.” (<ref target="X5425" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x2977">Revelation, 16 and 17
Dec. 1833</ref>,
in Doctrine and Covenants 97:4, 1835 ed. [D&amp;C 101:17–18]</ref>;
<ref target="X5424" pm:link-pointer-type="source"><ref pm:link-pointer-type="relatedDocument" target="x413">Revelation, 12 Oct.
1833</ref>,
in Doctrine and Covenants 94:4, 1835 ed. [D&amp;C 100:13]</ref>.)</note>
<lb/>and also, while in their <lb/>dispersion, that he would <lb/>provide for
them that <lb/>they perish not with <lb/>hunger nor cold. <lb/>And finally,
that God <lb/>in the name of Jesus <lb/>would gather his elect
<lb/>speedily<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">,</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice> and unveil <lb/>his face that his
<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">saints</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">Saints</reg></choice>
<pb xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5433" type="present" n="47" facs="6662_MS155_1_01_JSJ-1_047.jpg"/> might behold his glory and <lb/>dwell
with him<choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">;</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">.</reg></choice> Amen.</p> 
<ab xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5439" rend="noindent" type="journal-entry-divider"><date when-iso="1834-01-28">28 January 1834</date> • Tuesday</ab> 
<p xml:id="JSPPJ1_d1e5442" rend="indent">On the <date when-iso="1833-03-13">13th of March A.D. <lb/>1833</date>,
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="person" target="x4278" n="Hurlbut, Doctor Philastus"><choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">Docter P. </reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear"/></choice><supplied resp="WordConversion">Doctor
Philastus</supplied> Hurlbut</ref> <lb/>came to my house; I <lb/>conversed with
him consid<lb/>erably about the <choice><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Expanded">book</reg><reg resp="darowskijf" type="Clear">Book</reg></choice> of <lb/>Mormon. He was
<ref pm:link-pointer-type="topic" target="x4098">ordained</ref> <lb/>to the
